From 22b2fb31b0c2ace4074dabbb234a737f97822282 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Thu, 29 Oct 2026 17:35:22 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] FEX: two faster versions of D2R's 3D lighting table blur,
 binds take turns

fex30's version of D2R's compute shader 83e2eb4a6bf5f03b took 12-13 ms
per run on the Adreno 740 (was 30 ms): texture reads were 51 times
fewer, and the time now goes to instructions, about 12 per summed
product, as every thread loaded both factors from shared memory and
worked out their addresses for one cell.

Two new versions sum more cells per thread and read shared memory at
fixed offsets (both tables in one array, s2 slots in i order):
- 4 cells per thread (x and x + 4 by y and y + 4, local size 4x4x8):
  the two y cells share their s2 values and the two x cells their s1
  values. Host Turnip: inner loop ~5.8 instructions per product, 48
  registers, 4 waves per core.
- 2 cells per thread (y and y + 4, local size 8x4x8): ~7 instructions
  per product, 24 registers, 8 waves per core.
Both match the game's shader up to float rounding on lavapipe (16
table sizes and the fallback path, largest relative difference 5e-6).

VKD3D_FEX_SHADER_SWAP=alt (also the default when unset) gives the
pipeline both versions and each bind takes the next one in turn, so one
session times both on the device; 1 = 4 cells per thread only, 2 = 2
cells per thread only, 0 = the game's shader. The gpu report flags are
set at device creation, so FEX's '# vkd3d' line sees the swap (fex30
set them only at the first swapped pipeline).
